<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Go Shopping!</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> I Want Five Pineapples.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Facilitate basic peer interactions in group activities. Concept: Relate concepts to everyday scenarios.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can facilitate peer interactions in shopping activities with simple prompts (e.g., holds up an item and says, "How many apples do you want?"). Can relate shopping words to everyday scenarios with gestures (e.g., points to an apple and says, "I buy this."). Can support peers in understanding items by using visuals (e.g., pointing to three apples and saying, "three apples").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <!-- STAGE 1 -->
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Shopping Basket" game. The teacher shows flashcards of fruits (pineapple, apple, orange, banana) and numbers (one to ten). Students repeat the names and numbers. Teacher says, "I want three apples!" and students hold up three fingers and say "Apples!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models polite shopping requests and how to help a friend understand. Teacher holds up a flashcard of an apple and says, "I want two apples, please." Then, the teacher models mediation: "If my friend doesn't understand 'apple', I can point to the picture. Look, 'apple'!" (points clearly). The teacher also models the pronunciation of /b/ in "banana" and /p/ in "pineapple" by exaggerating the sounds and having students repeat chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up a banana flashcard: "Is this a pineapple?" (Expected answer: "No, it's a banana.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up five fingers: "How many oranges do I want?" (Expected answer: "Five oranges.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 2 -->
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue showing a shopping interaction. Two characters: a shopper and a seller. A third character (a child or an adult) can be seen helping to clarify something for the shopper. (e.g., Shopper: "I want four... (points to apples). Seller: "Four apples?" Helper: "Yes, four apples!" or points to the apples and counts). The dialogue uses "I want [number] [fruit], please." and "How many [fruit] do you want?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing quantity: Using polite expressions to count and describe objects while shopping (e.g., "I want three apples, please."). Requesting items: "Can I have two bananas, please?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are the people doing?" (Expected answer: "Shopping.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher reads simple statements, and students say "True" or "False" based on the dialogue. E.g., "The shopper wants ten bananas." (False, if the dialogue says five). "The seller has oranges." (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match a picture of a fruit with the number the shopper wants, based on the dialogue.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 3 -->
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Number + noun (e.g., "three bananas"), Present simple ("I want five pineapples."), Numbers (one, two, three...).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> pineapple, apple, orange, banana, money, cart, market, store, basket, buy, sell.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /b/ in "banana", /p/ in "pineapple". Blending phonemes for numbers (e.g., /s-ɪ-k-s/ in "six").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Expressing quantity ("I want three apples."), Requesting items ("Can I have two bananas, please?").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Fill-in-the-blanks:</b> Students complete sentences individually on a worksheet: "I want ___ (number) ___ (fruit), please." Teacher provides picture cues for numbers and fruits. (e.g., a picture of "3" and "apples" under the blanks).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Shopping Prompts with Mediation:</b> The teacher calls out a number and a fruit (e.g., "Four, oranges!"). Students say, "I want four oranges, please." Then, the teacher adds a "help" scenario: "Your friend says 'oranges' but points to pineapples. What can you do?" (Teacher guides students to suggest pointing to the correct picture or repeating the word slowly). Students practice saying "oranges" clearly, focusing on pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Help Your Shopper" Game:</b> Students work in pairs. One is the "Shopper," the other is the "Seller." The Shopper uses picture cards of fruits and number cards to make a request (e.g., "I want [card: 2] [card: bananas], please."). If the Seller looks confused or says the wrong item, the Shopper must *mediate* by pointing to the correct card, repeating the word slowly, or making a gesture (e.g., peeling a banana). They then switch roles.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 4 -->
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!" (Project 2: Shopping Role-Play)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Students perform their "Shopping Role-Play" in small groups (3 students per group). One student is the "Shopper," one is the "Seller/Cashier," and one is the "Helper/Mediator." The Helper's role is explicit: to assist either the Shopper or Seller if there's a misunderstanding, using gestures, pointing to realia/flashcards, or repeating words clearly. Students use toy money and fruit flashcards/drawings to make their market/store setup.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups set up their "mini-stores" with items (flashcards or plastic fruits) and toy money.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The Shopper approaches the Seller and requests items using "I want [number] [fruit], please." and "Can I have [number] [fruit], please?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The Seller responds, "How many [fruit] do you want?" and "Here you go!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The Helper/Mediator observes. If the Shopper forgets a word, or the Seller misunderstands the quantity, the Helper steps in to clarify: "Look, two pineapples!" (points to two pineapples), or "She wants *three* bananas." (emphasizes the number and points).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students rotate roles so everyone gets a chance to be the Shopper, Seller, and Helper.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 5 -->
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes each group during the "Shopping Role-Play" activity. Students present their role-play to the teacher or the rest of the class. The teacher focuses on how students facilitate interactions and use the target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /b/, /p/, and numbers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (fruits, money, buy, sell) correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target grammar ("I want [number] [noun].") correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clearly expresses quantity and requests items politely.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediation:</b> Facilitates peer interactions using simple prompts, gestures, or visuals (e.g., points, repeats slowly, prompts a friend).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates well with group members.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with two questions:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One thing I did well to help my friend understand was: (e.g., I pointed to the orange.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One thing that was easy when I was shopping was: (e.g., saying the numbers.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 6 -->
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was something fun you learned about shopping today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did you help your friends, or how did your friends help you during the shopping game?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in facilitating peer interaction during the role-play? (e.g., specific prompts, the "Helper" role, visual aids).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks, especially for students struggling with basic requests or clarifying items? (e.g., more practice with gestures, simplified vocabulary for mediating).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Let's Go Shopping!" theme. The next theme will introduce new vocabulary and scenarios, building on students' foundational interaction and mediation skills for basic communication.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>